Chapter: SubstringBounds
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `substring` traps on an out-of-range request. This file is the other half:
 every LEGAL request must still answer, including both boundaries, because
 a bounds check that refuses a legal call is worse than the hole it closed
 and `substring` is called from everywhere.

 The trap itself cannot be pinned here -- it is a UD2, so the guest dies and
 there is no output to compare. `codex/test/substring-over-read.codex` is
 that demonstration and it carries a `.fatal`, which means it is a compile
 check plus a manual read and nothing more. This file is the part a battery
 can actually fail on, so it is the one that has to be complete.

 The lattice is every start from 0 to the length, with every length that
 fits from that start. Zero length at every position is included, and so is
 the empty slice at the very end (start == length, length 0), which is the
 case a naive `start < length` check rejects.

Section: Subject

  s : Text = "abcde"

Section: Report

  row : Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (start) (len) =
    let r = substring s start len
    in " " & show start & "," & show len & "=[" & r & "]"

  from-start : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  from-start (start) (len) (room) (acc) =
    if len > room then acc
    else from-start start (len + 1) room (acc & row start len)

  walk : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  walk (start) (n) (acc) =
    if start > n then acc
    else walk (start + 1) n (acc & from-start start 0 (n - start) "")

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (walk 0 (text-length s) "")
    print-line-uni ("len=" & show (text-length (substring s 0 5)))
    print-line-uni ("empty-at-end=" & show (text-length (substring s 5 0)))
  end

